# driftwood.pwmodel - a long sea-broken spar, split along the grain.
#
# WHAT MAKES A SHAPE READ AS SEA-WORN DRIFTWOOD, AND WHAT SEVEN EARLIER VERSIONS
# GOT WRONG. The list below is ordered by how much each item moved the read in a
# render, not by how much work it was:
#
#   1. IT IS LONG AND THIN - BUT THAT IS NOT WHAT MAKES IT WOOD. This spar is 475
#      long and never more than 51 across. The version before this one was 330 long
#      and 120 across and read as a bone or an animal, so the ratio was raised to
#      about 10 to 1 - and the 10 to 1 version read as a bone TOO: a smooth pale
#      tapering rod is a bone, a spear shaft or a model-aircraft spar at any ratio.
#      Ratio buys you nothing on its own. What separates wood from bone is SPLITS
#      and LONGITUDINAL RELIEF (rules 3 and 5); the ratio only stops it reading as
#      a torso.
#
#   2. THE THICKNESS GOES DOWN THEN UP THEN DOWN. A shape that only gets thinner is
#      a spike. Read the `profile=` radii below: a flared butt of 25, a waist of
#      12, a collar back up to 18, a second waist, a club, then the break. That
#      column IS the model, and everything else on the part is a warp applied to it.
#      The rungs are ~9 apart and enumerated rather than sparse, because the grain
#      noise (rule 3) cannot show detail finer than the mesh it displaces and this
#      file no longer runs `subdivide` to buy that density. They were generated by
#      a script and pasted in as literal data, which is the format's own advice for
#      enumerable geometry.
#
#   3. THE SURFACE HAS TO RUN WITH THE GRAIN, AND THAT IS THE SINGLE BIGGEST WIN
#      IN THIS FILE. Isotropic `noise_deform` pebbles a limb and reads as a lumpy
#      sausage; the same magnitude spent on relief that RUNS ALONG the limb reads
#      as weathered wood. There is no anisotropic noise op, so:
#          stretch axis=z factor=0.25
#          noise_deform ...          # two octaves
#          stretch axis=z factor=4
#      Squash local Z, displace, put Z back: a feature the noise draws 12 across
#      comes back 12 across and 48 long. One line of setup per limb.
#      KNOW THE OTHER HALF OF THE SAME MULTIPLICATION. The unsquash multiplies the
#      AXIAL component of the displacement by the same factor, so any face whose
#      normal points along Z moves four times as far as you asked. On this model
#      that is the revolve's end caps: at these magnitudes a cap can be flung 17
#      units along the limb, and a cap that lands on the KEEP side of a break plane
#      hangs there as a flap. Every limb therefore carries SACRIFICIAL STOCK past
#      its break plane - the trunk profile runs to -288 and +282 for a piece whose
#      breaks are at -214 and +231 - so the wobbling cap is nowhere near the cut.
#      HONESTY ABOUT THIS ONE: the stock, and the long gentle end cones that
#      precede it, were added while chasing an open-mesh failure that turned out to
#      have a different cause entirely (see the `cylinder` note below). Neither was
#      ever shown to be load-bearing on its own. They are kept as cheap insurance
#      against arithmetic that is demonstrably real, not as a fix with evidence
#      behind it - if you are trimming this file, that is where to look first.
#
#   4. IT IS BLEACHED, AND THE PREVIEW IS WORTH FAR MORE THAN A STOP. The old bark
#      of (0.22, 0.20, 0.17) is the colour of a FRESH branch. But the correction
#      overshot hard: lifted to 0.62 the whole model came back porcelain-white with
#      no shading left in it, and read as bone china. Measured off two renders of
#      the same camera, the preview maps vertex albedo A to screen luminance
#      roughly L = 0.625 * A^0.37 - so brutally compressive that 1.8x of albedo is
#      1.25x of luminance. Consequences, both counter-intuitive:
#        * The body sits at 0.30-0.50, which lands near L 0.40-0.48. That IS pale
#          silver on screen. Do not chase it higher.
#        * COLOUR CANNOT CARRY A FEATURE. A tool tint 0.11 below the body is 0.06
#          on screen. Every dark fissure in this model is dark because it is a
#          shadowed hole, not because it is painted. Tool `color=` is there to stop
#          boolean faces rendering pure WHITE (see materials below), and for
#          nothing else.
#
#   5. SPLITS ARE THE SIGNAL, AND THEY BELONG ON THE BROAD FACES. Long splits
#      running with the grain are the one feature nothing else in nature has. Four
#      versions cut them and could not see them, and the reason was not depth or
#      width - it was WHICH FLANK. The section is flattened, so it has two broad
#      faces (local +/-Y) and two narrow edges (local +/-X), and every check in
#      those versions was cut into the top EDGE, where a 15-wide strip is all the
#      room there is. Moved onto the broad faces the same tool cuts 10 to 14 wide
#      and 5 to 9 deep and reads at whole-object framing. See THE GROOVE TOOL.
#      VISIBILITY WAS ONLY HALF THE PROBLEM, AND SOLVING IT EXPOSED THE OTHER HALF.
#      The move to the broad faces did exactly what it promised - the splits are
#      unmissable now at any framing - and the version that shipped it read WORSE
#      than the version that could not see them, because what became visible was
#      five straight-sided, square-ended rectangles. A feature you cannot see is a
#      wasted feature; a feature you can see that says "machined" is an active lie
#      about the material. Do not treat "it reads at whole-object framing" as the
#      finish line: shoot it and say what it reads AS. Both halves are needed, and
#      the second one is CUT ORDER.
#
#   6. THE ENDS ARE BROKEN, NOT ROUNDED - TESTED, AND THE RULE SURVIVED. The rule
#      used to read "EVERY END IS BROKEN; a rounded cap is the strongest wrong
#      signal a driftwood model can send", and it was challenged on the grounds
#      that surf abrades a fresh break away over years. So one revision rounded
#      four of the six ends: the profiles were domed over at radius 11-13 and the
#      tip slabs deleted. THE RENDER KILLED IT. A smooth dome on a smooth tapering
#      pale rod is a bone epiphysis - `dw_r1_lit.png` and `dw_r1_fork.png` came
#      back reading as a bleached limb bone, worse than the broken version, and
#      the off-axis bites meant to make the domes asymmetric read as eye sockets
#      and nostrils. The rule is restated rather than deleted, because the REASON
#      is now known and it is not "rounded is wrong":
#          A worn end still has to be ANGULAR AND DISCONTINUOUS with the shaft.
#          What reads as bone is smooth continuity, not curvature.
#      Five ends are therefore oblique breaks - four of them with rim gouges;
#      `stub_b` is cut by its break slab and nothing else, for the reason recorded
#      on that part. `stub_a` is the one blunt worn end, and it only works because
#      it is also FAT (its radii went up 30 per cent) and barely bent: the thin,
#      strongly bent, heavily smoothed version of the same limb read as a shark fin
#      in `dw_r5_fork.png`.
#      THIS IS NOW THE MODEL'S WORST FEATURE, AND IT IS AN HONEST BLOCKER. An
#      oblique plane through a round limb is an ELLIPSE - flat, clean-rimmed and
#      smooth - so on the two SMALL limbs, where no gouge fits, the break reads as a
#      sawn dowel end. Rendered close, `stub_b` is a length of cut plastic pipe and
#      the `knob` is a plug pushed into the trunk; both are exactly the failure the
#      `stub_b` comment says it was avoiding, and it is not avoided. The three
#      obvious ways out were each tried and each fails at this radius:
#        - a crater ellipsoid or a rim notch bores an 8-radius stub hollow, because
#          any tool big enough to bite is bigger than the section (see `stub_b`);
#        - rounding the end over in the profile gives a bone epiphysis (rule 6);
#        - the grain noise now runs after the break cut and does ripple the face,
#          but `stub_b` and `knob` carry magnitudes of 1.7 and 1.4 - sized against
#          their own diameter - and that is far too little to tear a rim. Raising
#          them is the one untried lever, and it trades against the shark-fin and
#          pebbling failures above, so it wants its own render pass.
#      The two LONG limbs do not have this problem: their breaks are wide enough to
#      carry gouges and enough noise, and they read as torn.
#
#   7. LEAVE A SPLIT LONG. Two of the long fissures were once cut as a row of short
#      segments, on the theory that a split with shoulders of sound wood between it
#      reads less mechanical. The opposite happened: `dw_r7_fork.png` came back
#      with a row of square-ended rectangles reading as ventilation louvres or a
#      magazine well, and the single unbroken 100-long split it replaced was better
#      in every way. Reverted, and the rule stands.
#      THE REASON GIVEN FOR IT WAS WRONG, THOUGH, AND IT COST A REVISION. The old
#      wording was "a box tool's flat ends are a feature at 100 long and a defect at
#      40", which says length dilutes the square end. It does not: rendered at 104
#      long the square end was just as visible, because it is an end cap either way
#      and the eye finds it whatever it is attached to. What was actually wrong with
#      the segmented version is that it had SIX square ends instead of two, in a row,
#      at a spacing that reads as a repeat. Length does not fix a square end; running
#      the grain noise after the cut does, by feathering it (see CUT ORDER).
#
# THE CORDUROY WAS NEVER GEOMETRY - AND THE JOKE IS THAT IT SHOULD HAVE BEEN.
# Four successive versions of this file wore a fine corrugation running the length
# of every limb, and four successive authors went looking for it in the mesh. It was
# blamed on `remesh_uniform`, which was removed; the banding stayed. It was blamed
# on `twist` over a 9-gon; `segments` went from 9 to 14 and twist from 52 to 16, and
# the banding stayed. A six-column probe finally isolated it - bare cone, cone +
# `subdivide`, cone + `twist`, cone + both, cone + `taper`, and the full deformer
# stack with `split_normals`, all in one render:
#     ALL SIX WERE CLEAN.
# The only thing the probe changed was the MATERIAL. The banding is the grid texture
# on `/Engine/EngineMaterials/DefaultMaterial`, tiled by this file's own
# `uv mode=cylindrical` at unit scale.
# That diagnosis is correct and the material binding stays. What the four authors
# got wrong was the CONCLUSION they drew from it: they removed the corrugation and
# left the limbs glassy. Weathered wood IS corrugated along the grain - the soft
# spring growth erodes and the hard rings stand proud - and once the fake one was
# gone nothing replaced it, which is most of why this model read as bone for five
# revisions. Rule 3 puts a real one back, as geometry, at a wavelength chosen
# against the mesh rather than inherited from somebody's texture.
#
# WHY ONE `revolve` PER LIMB AND NOT A CHAIN OF `cone`s
# The chain came first, and it left a visible step at every junction: wherever one
# cone ENDS with a radius larger than its neighbour's at that height, the union of
# the two has a shoulder, and a shoulder in the middle of a branch reads as a cut
# face. Matching the radii at every junction by hand is possible and is exactly the
# arithmetic a profile does for free. A `revolve` also makes rule 2 more legible,
# not less - the radii are one column instead of eight scattered over four lines -
# and, critically for the grooves below, it makes the surface radius at any height
# something you read off the source rather than solve for.
# `revolve` followed by `taper` USED TO BE an instant editor crash - access
# violation inside FFlareMeshOp, no diagnostic and no failed compile. The engine's
# space deformers read a normal-overlay element's parent vertex without checking it,
# and an element no triangle references answers -1. `bend`, `twist` and `taper` now
# free those elements first (see docs/pwmodel-format.md), so this is safe again;
# that crash is the only reason the file was ever written with cones. Each part
# still logs one benign PWMODEL_STAGE_WARNING from `taper` saying it freed two of
# them - that message is the fix reporting itself, not a defect.
#
# NO `subdivide`, AND THAT IS A REVERSAL. It used to run on the two long limbs to
# give the deformers loops in BOTH directions, because profile rungs refine along
# the axis only. The grain streaks need radial density more than they need PN
# tessellation, and `steps` buys that directly and for a quarter of the triangles:
# the trunk is `steps=28` over 60 rungs. Subdividing on top of that put the model at
# 16.9k triangles for no visible gain over the 12.5k this file now compiles to.
#
# THE GROOVE TOOL: A SPLIT IS A NARROW BOX YAWED 45 DEGREES.
# The capsule version compiled clean, cut to the depth the arithmetic promised, and
# was INVISIBLE in a shaded render. The reason is not depth, it is the RIM: a round
# tool cutting near-tangent meets the surface tangentially, so the groove wall
# leaves the skin at zero degrees and there is no shading break anywhere along it -
# `split_normals` has nothing to split. A 7-deep trench read as a soft dent you had
# to be told was there. A split in wood has a SHARP rim, so the tool needs a face
# that meets the surface steeply. That is a box, and the standing objection to boxes
# is real but is about WIDTH:
#   * A WIDE box fails outward. Once its far face ends up past the surface it stops
#     being a trench and becomes a slab cut: it takes the whole flank off and leaves
#     the far lip standing as a thin flat plate.
#   * Widening the old top-edge checks from 4 to 7 hit exactly that: the render came
#     back with a long flat SHELF milled down the top of the trunk and torn faceted
#     slivers along both of its lips. DO NOT RE-RUN THAT EXPERIMENT.
#   * THE ANSWER IS NOT A NARROWER BOX, IT IS A ROTATED ONE. `rotate=(0, 0, 45)` on
#     a square box makes its cross-section a DIAMOND, and a diamond has no face
#     parallel to the surface at all, so it cannot mill a shelf however wide it
#     gets. Its two walls meet the skin at 45 degrees, which is a real shading
#     break, and they converge to a sharp line at the bottom, which is what a split
#     looks like. Every fissure in this file is that tool EXCEPT ONE - `branch_main`
#     carries a `box size=(40, 3, 70)` that is a thin unrotated slab cutting a 3-wide,
#     ~6-deep knife slot. It is not a diamond and nothing in this file explains it;
#     it renders acceptably, so it is left alone and recorded rather than quietly
#     converted, but it is the first thing to reconcile if this section is ever
#     rewritten as a rule.
#   * THE DIAMOND FIXES THE SECTION AND NOT THE PLAN, WHICH IS WHERE THIS SPENT FOUR
#     REVISIONS. A diamond gives the trench a sharp V bottom and a 45-degree rim -
#     the whole of what this section set out to buy - and leaves it a PRISM: swept
#     along a straight line, so both walls are planes, both lips are straight lines
#     and both ends are the tool's own square caps. Rendered, that is a milled slot.
#     Nothing about the cross-section can fix it, and neither can `color=`; what
#     fixes it is running the grain noise AFTER the cut so the walls stop being
#     planar. See CUT ORDER.
#   * The arithmetic is one line. For a box of side s the half-diagonal is
#     a = 0.7071 * s, the tool spans [c - a, c + a] about its centre c on the axis
#     it is cutting into, and:
#         depth        = surface - (c - a)
#         opening      = 2 * (a - |surface - c|)
#     Both are read straight off the profile. c + a MUST clear the surface by a few
#     units or the tool grazes - see the health note below.
# The round tools keep the jobs they are right for: the rim gouges, where a soft
# tangent edge is what a worn hollow has. There the rule runs the other way - an
# ELLIPSOID narrows toward its ends, so one sized to straddle the surface at its
# middle is entirely INSIDE the wood near its tips, and a tool entirely inside the
# wood carves an internal void rather than a surface hollow.
#
# BORE A HOLE WITH A `cylinder`, NEVER WITH AN ELLIPSOID. This cost a full bisect.
# With the grain streaks switched on, the model came back
#     isClosed false, boundaryEdges 3, nonManifoldVertices 3, componentCount 6
# and removing the streak block cleared it, which made the streaks look like the
# culprit. They were not. Removing the two through-cuts cleared it just as well with
# the streaks left in: an ellipsoid is tangent to SOME plane everywhere round its
# rim, and against a surface the streak noise has just made non-convex that tangency
# finds a place to shear a sliver off as its own closed shell. A `cylinder` scaled
# to the same opening has straight walls that meet the skin steeply all the way
# round, and it is clean. The extra shell showed as `componentCount` 6 against five
# parts long before it showed as an open boundary - READ THAT FIELD, it is the early
# warning, and it is not one of the two gate fields.
#
# `remove_degenerates` OPENS THE MESH AT ITS DEFAULT SETTING. Added to two parts to
# clear a one-triangle PWMODEL_DEGENERATE_GEOMETRY warning, it returned
# `isClosed false, boundaryEdges 15` on exactly those two parts: `mode` defaults to
# `repair_or_delete` and a deleted triangle leaves a hole nothing fills. If you ever
# need it here, it is `mode=repair_or_skip`. The warning it was chasing went to zero
# on its own once the grain-streak magnitudes went up.
#
# CUT ORDER: EVERY BOOLEAN RUNS FIRST, AND THE GRAIN NOISE RUNS AFTER THEM.
# The old rule was "near-tangent tools before the deformers, half-spaces after",
# because a flat break face bent afterwards stops being flat. The first half is
# still right and is why every fissure is cut against the raw column, where the
# surface radius is a number in the source rather than wherever `taper` and `bend`
# happened to leave it - two versions shipped a PWMODEL_BOOLEAN_NO_EFFECT by aiming
# at a deformed limb, and that is an error which aborts the part, not a warning.
# The second half turned out to be a rule about SIZE, not about half-spaces. Measure
# it: `bend angle=20 extent=235` is a radius of curvature of about 673, so across a
# break face 40 wide the sagitta is 40^2 / (8 * 673) = 0.07 units, and the twist
# shears it by another half unit. That is invisible. Cutting the breaks before the
# deformers costs nothing and buys the thing that actually matters here - the tip
# and butt slabs no longer have to be aimed at a limb whose position depends on two
# bends, a twist and a vector noise. The tip slab used to be re-derived by hand
# every time an angle changed; now it is a plane through a z you can read off the
# profile. Keep half-spaces after the deformers only where the face is a large
# fraction of the bend's extent.
#
# THE GRAIN-STREAK BLOCK IS A DEFORMER TOO, AND IT USED TO RUN BEFORE THE BOOLEANS.
# That was the bug behind this file's worst render, and it hid inside the paragraph
# above: the section claimed "every fissure is cut against the raw column, where the
# surface radius is a number in the source", and it was not - the stretch/noise/
# stretch block ran first, so every tool was in fact aimed at a surface displaced by
# up to +/-7. The block now runs immediately before `taper`, which makes the claim
# true for the first time and, far more visibly, FIXES THE FISSURES:
#   a box swept along a straight line has PLANAR walls. One plane is one normal is
#   one flat tone across the whole feature, with dead-straight lips and square ends
#   where the tool's own end caps land. That is a sticker, and no `color=` can save
#   it - the materials block below already worked out that "a flat-coloured flat
#   face with a hard straight boundary is what a sticker is" and then treated it as
#   a colour problem. It is a GEOMETRY problem. Rendered at whole-object framing the
#   five trunk fissures came back as tan rectangles with square ends, reading as
#   milled slots or strips of masking tape on a white tube.
#   Running the grain noise AFTER the cuts displaces the trench walls and lips along
#   with everything else, so the walls stop being planes, the lips wander, the width
#   opens and closes, and the square ends feather out. Same tools, same numbers, one
#   block moved: 12,790 -> 12,518 triangles, health unchanged.
# The aiming arithmetic below is unaffected, because it was always written against
# the profile rather than against the noised surface - it just describes reality now.
# `c + a` still has to clear the PROFILE radius, and every tool does, by 2.4 at the
# tightest (the -X channel at z where the profile stands to 18.8).
#
# WHICH FLANK IS WHICH is worth deriving before aiming anything, and is not a guess.
# The part rotate sends trunk-local +X to mesh (0.102, 0.022, -0.995) - almost
# straight DOWN - so local -X points UP, and local +Y lands on mesh
# (-0.208, 0.978, 0), which is where the preview camera has to stand (see below).
# The first version of the first groove sat at local +X and was cut perfectly, on
# the underside, where nothing would ever see it. The current file puts fissures on
# -X (up), +Y (camera) and -Y (the far broad face), which is also just what a piece
# fissured on every side looks like.
#
# HOW `bend` ACTUALLY MOVES THE LIMB, MEASURED OFF THE COMPILED BOUNDS
# `bend` deflects purely in the local YZ plane - an elevation down local X of a bent
# limb is indistinguishable from a straight one. It bows the MIDDLE and leaves the
# ends where they were: for `angle=14 extent=235` the compiled bounds came back
# y = -11.9 .. 25.8, i.e. a waist pushed out to y = +19, and the deflection at
# height z is 19 * (1 - (z / 235)^2), scaling linearly with the angle. The tip
# TANGENT ends up about TWO THIRDS of `angle` off local +Z.
# So a tip cut is ROLLED about X, never pitched about Y. Every cut in this file was
# originally pitched, which tilts the plane in the one plane the bend does not use:
# the plane met the swung tip nearly edge-on and peeled the limb lengthwise into a
# wide flat plate with a knife edge.
# Under roll t, local +Z lands on (0, sin t, cos t), so a slab 300 deep whose face
# should pass through P is centred at P + 150 * (0, sin t, cos t). That identity is
# what every break box below is positioned by. Now that the slabs run BEFORE the
# deformers the tangent correction has dropped out of the arithmetic entirely, and
# the roll is simply how oblique you want the break: 34 degrees at the trunk tip,
# 30 at the butt, both far enough off perpendicular to give a long torn oval rather
# than the disc a saw leaves.
#
# THE SECOND BEND IS THE SILHOUETTE. `bend angle=20 extent=235` bows the whole spar;
# a second `bend angle=-30 symmetric_extents=false lower_extent=40
# bidirectional=false` bends only what is above z = -40 back the other way and
# leaves the butt third rigidly alone. That is an S-curve for two lines, and because
# the butt does not move, its break arithmetic is unaffected. A single big bend just
# makes a banana.
#
# THE AXIS WANDER IS A VECTOR NOISE, NOT A SURFACE NOISE.
# `noise_deform ... apply_along_normal=false` displaces by the 3D noise vector
# instead of along the normal, so at a wavelength of 130 units it shifts whole
# sections of the limb sideways: the AXIS wanders and the skin does not get bumpy.
# It runs last, after every boolean, so it cannot break a cut, and at magnitude 17
# on the trunk it is most of what stops the spar reading as a straight rod.
#
# EXTENTS ARE SYMMETRIC HALF-EXTENTS
# `bend`, `twist` and `taper` are pure functions of local Z and the deform spans
# [-extent, +extent]. Every limb is authored standing along +Z, centred on z = 0,
# and carried into place by `rotate=`/`at=` on the part header; because a part pivots
# about its part-local ORIGIN, `at=` is the joint position plus half the limb length
# along the limb's own axis. With roll held at 0, a part's `rotate=(0, pitch, yaw)`
# sends local +Z to (-sin(pitch)cos(yaw), -sin(pitch)sin(yaw), cos(pitch)) - that
# identity is how every `at=` below was computed, and it is why each limb root lands
# ON the trunk centreline instead of near it. The trunk axis it lands on is
# A = (0.9727, 0.2068, 0.1045); a joint at distance t along the trunk is t * A.
# Adding sacrificial stock to a profile does NOT move any of this: `at=` is a fixed
# translation and the rotation is about the part-local origin, so lengthening a
# profile only adds geometry the break slab then removes.
#
# THE FORK ANGLES WERE SOLVED, NOT TYPED. Each limb direction is
# L = cos(f) * A + sin(f) * U for a unit U perpendicular to A, then solved back into
# (pitch, yaw) through the identity above. `f` is the thing worth choosing: a fork
# at 52 degrees off the trunk is a branch, and the 72 degrees the previous version
# happened to land on is a limb sticking out of a body.
#
# HOW TO PHOTOGRAPH THIS THING, BECAUSE HALF THE FALSE VERDICTS CAME FROM RENDERS.
#   * The preview key light is near AZIMUTH 110 and above - measured by orbiting,
#     not inferred from one frame. A camera in the -Y half-space photographs the
#     shadow side and this model comes back very dark. Stand near azimuth 110; the
#     +Y broad face is at mesh azimuth 102, so the flank this model has to show and
#     the flank the key lights are within 8 degrees of each other.
#   * THE SCENE HAS FILL, and the note that used to sit here saying it had "next to
#     no fill" was wrong - it was a COLD CAPTURE misread, the exact trap the bullet
#     two below describes. On a warm frame `minLuminance` is ~0.02-0.05 and
#     `litPixelFraction` is 1.0; on the first shot into a fresh window it is 0 and
#     the shadow side reads as pure black. Do not conclude a face is unlightable
#     from a first capture.
#   * Pin the exposure - `exposure:{mode:"fixed", ev100:-1}` - or two shots of the
#     same mesh are not comparable.
#   * PIN IS NOT ENOUGH: THE FIRST CAPTURE INTO A FRESHLY OPENED ASSET EDITOR IS
#     ABOUT A STOP DARK. Measured on this model, identical camera and identical
#     pinned exposure, whole-image mean luminance 0.206 on the first shot and 0.371
#     on every shot after it, with `warmup.settled` reporting true both times. The
#     preview scene's environment has not converged yet. Take a throwaway frame
#     first and only compare warmed-up ones, or you will read a lighting change as
#     a modelling change - which happened here once.
#   * A steep three-quarter shot with all 475 units in frame is the honest test but
#     it is the WORST-looking one, because it shows the broad top faces and hides
#     the fissures on the sides. A low camera (pitch about -8) is where this shape
#     reads best and is worth shooting too; do not let it be the only shot.

pwmodel 0

materials {
    # NO TEXTURED MATERIAL, EVER, ON THIS MODEL. See THE CORDUROY WAS NEVER
    # GEOMETRY above: a stock grid texture is what four versions of this file
    # mistook for a mesh defect. The master bound here carries no texture at all -
    # its grain is ALU Perlin, with no sample, no import step and no mip chain -
    # which removes the trap rather than working around it.
    #
    # THE COROLLARY THAT USED TO SIT HERE IS NOW FALSE, AND THE `uv` OPS BELOW ARE
    # LOAD-BEARING AGAIN. This paragraph used to end "this model has NO surface
    # variation except geometry: there is nothing else to carry it", and that was
    # the whole defect: bound to a flat matte dielectric reading a flat per-face
    # vertex colour, a smooth revolve renders as PAINTED CLAY, which is exactly
    # what came back from review. Vertex colour cannot fix it - the overlay stores
    # one value per triangle CORNER, so it can say which region a face belongs to
    # and nothing finer, and grain is sub-face detail by definition.
    # `M_PwModelExample_WeatheredWood` is the missing half. It reads UV0 - so
    # `uv channel=0 mode=cylindrical scale=(0.02, 0.02)` on every part below is now
    # sampled rather than ignored, and changing it changes the render.
    #
    # THE GRAIN AXIS IS V, AND THE TWO UV AXES DO NOT SCALE ALIKE. Cylindrical
    # projection puts U around the limb and V along it. U spans about 0..1 per full
    # turn whatever `scale=` says; V is world distance DIVIDED by `scale=`, so at
    # 0.02 it runs 50 UV units per world unit and this 475-long spar spans about
    # 24,000. The material's `GrainAcross` is therefore read as fibres-per-turn and
    # `GrainAlong` as 1/UV. If a future edit changes `scale=` here, `GrainAlong`
    # and `CheckAlong` on the material must be re-derived; `GrainAcross` need not.
    #
    # TWO SLOTS, ONE MATERIAL, AND THE SPLIT IS STILL `color=`. Sea-bleached wood
    # and the pale wood a fresh break exposes are the same substance at two stages
    # of weathering, and that stays true with a procedural surface on it: there is
    # no second shading behaviour here to justify a second instance, and inventing
    # one would be the exact mistake this file has already made once. The material
    # separates the two stages from the vertex colour it is already given, by
    # SATURATION - it bleaches a near-neutral body tint hard and leaves a warm,
    # saturated one alone - so `Heartwood`'s honey tools survive the silvering
    # without a slot, an instance or a parameter of their own.
    #
    # THE OLD RULE HERE WAS "AN EXAMPLE MUST NOT SHIP A VALUE BREAK", AND IT WAS
    # OVERCORRECTED. It was written after a version put the biggest fork on a
    # second, much brighter STOCK material: that read as two objects welded
    # together and no amount of geometry work recovered it. The lesson was real but
    # it was about the wrong thing - what failed was borrowing an unrelated engine
    # asset with its own value and its own texture, not the idea of a lighter
    # region. `Heartwood` is on `stub_b` alone, the one limb that carries a FRESH
    # break, and its tools are the only warm tint on the model.
    #
    # WHAT CARRIES THE BREAK COLOUR IS EVERY `subtract` TOOL. A boolean leaves the
    # TOOL'S surface behind as the new face, and that face takes the TOOL'S
    # `color=`. On this model those faces are every fissure, both bores and every
    # break plane - every place the wood is actually opened. Untinted they render
    # pure WHITE, and the bright slivers along the spar that read as a lighting bug
    # were exactly that. `set_vertex_color set_all=true` does NOT reach them -
    # tried on all five parts, the white survived unchanged; the tint has to be on
    # the tool op.
    #
    # BUT DO NOT ASK THE TINT TO CARRY A FEATURE, AND THIS IS A CORRECTION. A
    # version tinted the tool faces honey (0.72, 0.58, 0.36) against dark bark to
    # make the splits legible, then a later one tinted them dark against a bleached
    # body for the same reason. BOTH READ AS DECALS: `dw_r1_fork.png` came back
    # with the fissures looking like strips of masking tape laid on a glassy white
    # tube, because a flat-coloured flat face with a hard straight boundary is what
    # a sticker is. The tool tints here now sit close to the body value
    # ((0.19, 0.17, 0.135) against a body of 0.30-0.50) and do nothing but keep the
    # boolean faces off pure white. What makes a fissure dark is that it is a deep
    # narrow hole. See rule 4 for the measured reason colour cannot win here: the
    # preview maps albedo to luminance as roughly L = 0.625 * A^0.37, so a 0.11
    # albedo difference is 0.06 on screen.
    # AND NEUTRALISING THE TINT DID NOT STOP THEM READING AS DECALS - that was
    # measured later and it is worth being blunt about, because this paragraph used
    # to imply the problem was closed. The diagnosis in it is exactly right and its
    # own words give the fix away: "a flat-coloured FLAT FACE with a hard straight
    # boundary". Two of those three are geometry. Toning the colour down removed the
    # first and left a flat face with a hard straight boundary, which is still a
    # sticker - and that is how it rendered until the cut order changed. See CUT
    # ORDER; the tints are right and were never the lever.
    #
    # THE BODY VARIES PER LIMB, over about 0.20 of albedo, so the object is not one
    # flat tone: 0.30 on the trunk up to 0.50 on the knob. `stub_a` was once the
    # DARKEST at 0.235 and, being an upright limb on the shadow side, it rendered
    # as a black blade - it is now 0.40, above the trunk, so it catches light.
    Bark      = "/Game/PinWrightExamples/Materials/M_PwModelExample_WeatheredWood"
    Heartwood = "/Game/PinWrightExamples/Materials/M_PwModelExample_WeatheredWood"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The spar. 475 of visible wood between its two breaks, authored along +Z with
# sacrificial stock at both ends (see rule 3), laid almost flat along +X by the
# part rotate.
#
# THE PROFILE IS THE MODEL, and it is enumerated at ~9-unit rungs rather than the
# 25 it used to use. Two reasons, both mechanical: the grain-streak noise samples
# at wavelengths down to about 8 units and cannot show detail finer than the mesh,
# and `subdivide` - which used to supply that density - is gone. The control shape
# it was generated from, radius against z:
#     -230  -204  -188  -156  -124   -92   -60   -26    34    98   158   194   224
#      15.5   24   25.5  24.5  16.5  12.2    18    12  14.6  18.6  12.6  16.2  13.2
# A flared butt, a first waist, a collar, a second waist, a long swell to the club
# at +194, and away to the tip break. Each rung then carries +/-0.25 of deterministic
# jitter so the surface is not a clean spline.
#
# THE WIDEST POINT IS NOT AT EITHER END, AND THAT IS A CORRECTION. An older profile
# ran 23 at the butt straight down to the waist, so the thickest section in the
# model was the one the butt slab cuts through - and a 46-wide flat oval is then the
# single largest feature on the object. It read as a sawn flute end, and no amount
# of chipping at its rim fixed a face that big. Cut faces should be the small ends
# of a shape, always.
#
# `scale=(1, 0.68, 1)`, AND IT USED TO BE 0.55. The section is still flattened -
# that is a real property of a piece that has been rolled in surf - but 0.55 made
# it a RIBBON, and a ribbon has no material in its thin direction to cut a fissure
# into. That is the constraint that kept every earlier check pinned to the narrow
# top edge at 4 wide. At 0.68 the broad faces are 17 to 34 across and take a
# 10-to-14-wide, 5-to-9-deep V without coming near punching through. Flatness that
# forbids the feature it exists to enable is not worth having.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
part trunk rotate=(0, -84, 12) {
    revolve profile=[(0, -288), (6, -274), (11, -262), (15, -252), (16.11, -242.9), (15.78, -233.9), (17.66, -224.8), (20.71, -215.7), (23.45, -206.6), (24.7, -197.6), (25.26, -188.5), (24.33, -179.4), (22.93, -170.4), (23.9, -161.3), (23.79, -152.2), (21.75, -143.1), (19.15, -134.1), (16.56, -125), (14.59, -115.9), (12.94, -106.9), (12.46, -97.8), (12.91, -88.7), (15.28, -79.6), (16.83, -70.6), (17.92, -61.5), (16.25, -52.4), (13.78, -43.4), (12.76, -34.3), (11.82, -25.2), (12.57, -16.1), (12.42, -7.1), (13.15, 2), (13.31, 11.1), (13.97, 20.1), (14.51, 29.2), (14.87, 38.3), (15.28, 47.4), (15.98, 56.4), (16.79, 65.5), (17.57, 74.6), (17.7, 83.6), (18.42, 92.7), (18.28, 101.8), (17.68, 110.9), (16.89, 119.9), (15.24, 129), (14.08, 138.1), (13.19, 147.1), (12.67, 156.2), (13.23, 165.3), (14.32, 174.4), (15.44, 183.4), (16.16, 192.5), (15.6, 201.6), (14.83, 210.6), (13.77, 219.7), (12.05, 228.8), (10.55, 237.9), (9.19, 246.9), (8, 256), (5, 268), (0, 282)] steps=28 scale=(1, 0.68, 1) material="Bark" color=(0.30, 0.29, 0.265, 1)

    # --- the fissures, cut against the RAW column, before any deformer ---------
    # One on the top EDGE (-X), two into the +Y broad face, two into -Y. Every one
    # is a box yawed 45 degrees, so its cross-section is a diamond with no face
    # parallel to the surface - see THE GROOVE TOOL. The grain-streak block that
    # used to sit above these now runs after them, which is what stops each trench
    # wall being a single flat plane - see CUT ORDER. Read them as
    # (centre, halfDiagonal a = 0.7071 * size) against the profile:
    #   -X  c 14.86  a 6.36  z   -3..115   surface R 12.4..18.8  ->  4.8..9.9 deep
    #   +Y  c 15     a 8.49  z -197..-155  surface 0.68R 15..17.2 ->  8.5..10.7
    #   +Y  c 12.6   a 7.99  z    6..110   surface 0.68R  9..12.8 ->  4.4..8.2
    #   -Y  c 11.8   a 7.50  z -124..-52   surface 0.68R 8.5..12.2 -> 4.2..7.9
    #   -Y  c 10.5   a 6.51  z  147..205   surface 0.68R 8.6..11   -> 4.6..7.0
    # c + a must clear the surface by a few units at EVERY z the tool spans, and it
    # is the ENDS of a long tool that catch you out: the -X channel first ran from
    # z = -9 to 141, where the profile narrows to 12.4 and c + a cleared the
    # surface by 1.3, which against a streaked surface is a grazing contact. It was
    # pulled back to -3..115 on suspicion of shearing slivers. It was not in fact
    # the cause of that failure, but a tool that only just reaches is worth nothing
    # anyway - it cuts a nick, not a split.
    subtract {
        box size=(9, 9, 118) at=(-14.86, 3, 56) rotate=(0, 0, 45) color=(0.19, 0.17, 0.135, 1)
    }
    subtract {
        box size=(12, 12, 42) at=(-6, 15, -176) rotate=(0, 0, 45) color=(0.19, 0.17, 0.135, 1)
    }
    subtract {
        box size=(11.3, 11.3, 104) at=(4, 12.6, 58) rotate=(0, 0, 45) color=(0.19, 0.17, 0.135, 1)
    }
    subtract {
        box size=(10.6, 10.6, 72) at=(-4, -11.8, -88) rotate=(0, 0, 45) color=(0.19, 0.17, 0.135, 1)
    }
    subtract {
        box size=(9.2, 9.2, 58) at=(3, -10.5, 176) rotate=(0, 0, 45) color=(0.19, 0.17, 0.135, 1)
    }

    # The rot cavity, bored right through the thin axis near the root flare.
    # A CYLINDER, not an ellipsoid: an ellipsoid meets the surface tangentially
    # somewhere along its rim, and against a streaked surface that tangency is
    # what shears a sliver off as its own shell.
    subtract {
        cylinder radius=1 height=68 segments=16 scale=(9, 17, 1) rotate=(90, 12, 0) at=(-2, -1, -150) color=(0.19, 0.17, 0.135, 1)
    }
    # A second, smaller bore far up the spar.
    subtract {
        cylinder radius=1 height=48 segments=14 scale=(4.5, 9, 1) rotate=(90, -9, 0) at=(1, 0, 120) color=(0.19, 0.17, 0.135, 1)
    }

    # --- the butt break, and the sacrificial stock beyond it -------------------
    subtract {
        box size=(420, 420, 300) at=(0, -75, -343.9) rotate=(-150, 0, 0) color=(0.35, 0.30, 0.245, 1)
    }
    subtract {
        sphere radius=1 subdivisions=12 scale=(7, 5, 11) at=(13, -3, -211) color=(0.35, 0.30, 0.245, 1)
    }
    subtract {
        sphere radius=1 subdivisions=12 scale=(5, 4, 9) at=(-14, 6, -216) color=(0.35, 0.30, 0.245, 1)
    }

    # --- the tip break ---------------------------------------------------------
    subtract {
        box size=(300, 300, 200) at=(0, 55.9, 313.9) rotate=(34, 0, 0) color=(0.35, 0.30, 0.245, 1)
    }
    subtract {
        sphere radius=1 subdivisions=12 scale=(4.5, 4.5, 9) at=(11, -3, 229) color=(0.35, 0.30, 0.245, 1)
    }
    subtract {
        sphere radius=1 subdivisions=12 scale=(4, 4, 8) at=(-9, 5, 225) color=(0.35, 0.30, 0.245, 1)
    }

    # --- THE GRAIN STREAKS ----------------------------------------------------
    # Squash local Z, run the noise, put Z back. A feature the noise draws round
    # the limb comes back four times longer along it, so the relief runs WITH the
    # grain instead of pebbling the surface.
    # ITS POSITION HERE - after every boolean, before every space deformer - is
    # load-bearing in both directions and is the single most consequential line
    # ordering in the file. After the booleans, so it ripples the fissure walls and
    # lips and they stop reading as milled slots. Before `taper`/`bend`/`twist`, so
    # the fissures above are still aimed at profile radii. See CUT ORDER.
    stretch axis=z factor=0.25
    noise_deform magnitude=4.6 frequency=0.072 seed=13
    noise_deform magnitude=2.4 frequency=0.13 seed=31
    stretch axis=z factor=4

    taper flare=(7, 0) extent=235

    bend angle=20 extent=235
    bend angle=-30 extent=240 symmetric_extents=false lower_extent=40 bidirectional=false
    twist angle=22 extent=235

    # Vector noise, NOT along-normal: it shifts whole sections of the limb
    # sideways, so the AXIS wanders instead of the skin getting bumpy.
    noise_deform magnitude=17 frequency=0.0075 seed=5 apply_along_normal=false

    uv channel=0 mode=cylindrical split_angle=40 scale=(0.02, 0.02)
    split_normals split_angle=30
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The fork. Leaves the trunk at t = +60, at 52 degrees off the trunk axis, and runs
# 223 of visible wood plus stock. Thinner than the trunk at the joint on purpose - a
# fork as thick as its parent reads as a second object, which is what the first
# draft of this limb did. Its butt cap is buried inside the trunk (parts are
# APPENDED, not unioned), which is also why its stock runs to -130: the grain-streak
# unsquash throws that cap around by up to 11 and it has to stay inside the trunk.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
part branch_main rotate=(0, -41.55, -23.4) at=(128.4, -17.9, 92.4) {
    revolve profile=[(0, -130), (12, -128), (12.48, -120.4), (12.64, -112.8), (12.06, -105.2), (11.28, -97.6), (10.48, -90.1), (10.07, -82.5), (10.66, -74.9), (11.55, -67.3), (12.25, -59.7), (11.64, -52.1), (10.61, -44.5), (10.03, -36.9), (9.19, -29.4), (9.44, -21.8), (9.86, -14.2), (10.06, -6.6), (10.58, 1), (11.06, 8.6), (11.54, 16.2), (12, 23.8), (12.85, 31.4), (12.81, 38.9), (12.4, 46.5), (11.73, 54.1), (11.04, 61.7), (11.16, 69.3), (11.87, 76.9), (12.38, 84.5), (12.16, 92.1), (11.42, 99.6), (10.83, 107.2), (10.05, 114.8), (9, 122.4), (8, 130), (0, 132)] steps=22 scale=(1, 0.62, 1) material="Bark" color=(0.40, 0.39, 0.36, 1)

    subtract {
        box size=(40, 3, 70) at=(-25.5, -1, -55) rotate=(0, -1.6, 0) color=(0.19, 0.17, 0.135, 1)
    }
    subtract {
        box size=(8, 8, 82) at=(-12, 2, 34) rotate=(0, 0, 45) color=(0.19, 0.17, 0.135, 1)
    }
    subtract {
        box size=(9.6, 9.6, 108) at=(2, 9.8, 4) rotate=(0, 0, 45) color=(0.19, 0.17, 0.135, 1)
    }
    subtract {
        box size=(9.2, 9.2, 66) at=(-2, -9.4, -74) rotate=(0, 0, 45) color=(0.19, 0.17, 0.135, 1)
    }

    subtract {
        box size=(200, 200, 200) at=(0, -46.9, 196.3) rotate=(-28, 0, 0) color=(0.35, 0.30, 0.245, 1)
    }
    subtract {
        sphere radius=1 subdivisions=12 scale=(4, 4, 8) at=(9, 3, 108) color=(0.35, 0.30, 0.245, 1)
    }
    subtract {
        sphere radius=1 subdivisions=10 scale=(3.5, 3.5, 7) at=(-7, -4, 104) color=(0.35, 0.30, 0.245, 1)
    }

    stretch axis=z factor=0.25
    noise_deform magnitude=3.4 frequency=0.09 seed=23
    noise_deform magnitude=1.8 frequency=0.15 seed=41
    stretch axis=z factor=4

    taper flare=(4, 0) extent=115
    bend angle=-26 extent=115
    twist angle=-20 extent=115

    noise_deform magnitude=9 frequency=0.014 seed=11 apply_along_normal=false

    uv channel=0 mode=cylindrical split_angle=40 scale=(0.02, 0.02)
    split_normals split_angle=30
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The second fork, near the butt at t = -120, 90 long - and the one the surf has
# rubbed down. This is the ONE limb whose far end is worn blunt rather than broken,
# and rule 6 explains why it is allowed to be: it is FAT (radii up 30 per cent from
# the version that read as a shark fin), barely bent (8, not 18) and carries only a
# single-iteration `relax` in place of the `smooth` that used to be here - enough to
# take the facets off, not enough to dome it - so it is a blunt lump rather than a
# smooth tapering dome. (`relax` and `smooth` are different ops; this limb is still
# smoothed, just far less.) It is also
# the lightest limb after the knob, because it stands up on the shadow side and a
# dark upright blade is exactly what it used to look like.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
part stub_a rotate=(0, -32.9, 52.3) at=(-101.7, -5.5, 25.3) {
    revolve profile=[(0, -45), (14.3, -45), (13.3, -39.2), (12.4, -33.3), (11.8, -27.5), (11.3, -21.7), (11.4, -15.8), (12.4, -10), (11.4, -4.2), (10.7, 1.7), (11.1, 7.5), (11.4, 13.3), (12.1, 19.2), (12.5, 25), (12.3, 30.8), (11.1, 36.7), (7.5, 42.5), (0, 45)] steps=18 scale=(1, 0.72, 1) material="Bark" color=(0.40, 0.39, 0.37, 1)

    subtract {
        sphere radius=1 subdivisions=10 scale=(7, 7, 6) at=(-8, 5, 40) color=(0.19, 0.17, 0.135, 1)
    }
    subtract {
        box size=(6.4, 6.4, 52) at=(1, 8.5, 4) rotate=(0, 0, 45) color=(0.19, 0.17, 0.135, 1)
    }

    stretch axis=z factor=0.25
    noise_deform magnitude=2.4 frequency=0.10 seed=59
    stretch axis=z factor=4

    taper flare=(3, 0) extent=45
    bend angle=8 extent=45
    twist angle=10 extent=45

    relax iterations=1 strength=0.25

    uv channel=0 mode=cylindrical split_angle=40 scale=(0.02, 0.02)
    split_normals split_angle=45
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The snapped stub: THE one fresh break on the piece, and the only place the
# second material slot is used - a fresh break is where heartwood shows.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
part stub_b rotate=(0, -130, -38.2) at=(145.1, 12.2, -6.3) {
    revolve profile=[(0, -31), (10, -31), (9.27, -25.4), (8.56, -19.8), (7.83, -14.2), (7.68, -8.5), (7.47, -2.9), (7.07, 2.7), (7.62, 8.3), (7.89, 13.9), (8.46, 19.5), (7.89, 25.2), (7.32, 30.8), (6.93, 36.4), (6.4, 42), (0, 44)] steps=18 scale=(1, 0.82, 1) material="Heartwood" color=(0.47, 0.41, 0.33, 1)

    # A long oblique tear, not a disc: roll 30 puts the plane 30 degrees off
    # perpendicular. NOTHING ELSE cuts this end. A crater ellipsoid plus a rim
    # notch were both tried here and both bored the stub hollow - at 8 radius there
    # is not enough section for a tool to take a bite without going right through,
    # and the result read as a length of cut plastic pipe in three renders running.
    subtract {
        box size=(160, 160, 140) at=(0, 35, 80.6) rotate=(30, 0, 0) color=(0.66, 0.53, 0.34, 1)
    }

    stretch axis=z factor=0.25
    noise_deform magnitude=1.7 frequency=0.11 seed=71
    stretch axis=z factor=4

    taper flare=(2.5, 0) extent=31
    bend angle=-12 extent=31
    twist angle=20 extent=31

    uv channel=0 mode=cylindrical split_angle=40 scale=(0.02, 0.02)
    split_normals split_angle=30
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The knob: a knot that never grew out, on the far side of the trunk at t = -40. It
# exists to stop the three long pieces reading as a limbed figure - a fourth stump
# at an unhelpful angle is what wood does and what an animal does not. Round in
# section (0.92, not 0.7) and cut off SHORT at z = -4: flattened, long and bent, it
# read as a fin.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
part knob rotate=(0, -60.3, 92.1) at=(-39.5, 9.1, 5.7) {
    revolve profile=[(0, -22), (9.5, -22), (8.84, -16.8), (8.84, -11.6), (8.96, -6.4), (8.72, -1.2), (8.56, 4), (8.55, 9.2), (8.39, 14.4), (7.85, 19.6), (7.44, 24.8), (7, 30), (0, 32)] steps=16 scale=(1, 0.92, 1) material="Bark" color=(0.50, 0.485, 0.455, 1)

    subtract {
        box size=(120, 120, 140) at=(0, 43.1, 51.2) rotate=(38, 0, 0) color=(0.35, 0.30, 0.245, 1)
    }
    subtract {
        sphere radius=1 subdivisions=10 scale=(3, 3, 5) at=(6, -3, -4) color=(0.35, 0.30, 0.245, 1)
    }

    stretch axis=z factor=0.25
    noise_deform magnitude=1.4 frequency=0.12 seed=97
    stretch axis=z factor=4

    taper flare=(2, 0) extent=22
    bend angle=3 extent=22
    twist angle=10 extent=22

    uv channel=0 mode=cylindrical split_angle=40 scale=(0.02, 0.02)
    split_normals split_angle=30
}

# Explicit elements, not `auto`: a forked spar is five lines, and one capsule per
# limb beats any hull decomposition of the whole for both cost and fit.
# Element at= / rotate= are in MESH space - after every part transform - so each
# capsule reuses its limb's part rotate= verbatim, and `height` is TOTAL height
# including the caps (the compiler converts to FKSphylElem::Length itself). Each
# capsule is centred on the VISIBLE span of its limb, which is shorter than the
# authored profile: every profile carries sacrificial stock past its break plane.
collision {
    complexity = simple_and_complex
    capsule radius=30 height=450 at=(8, 2, 1) rotate=(0, -84, 12)
    capsule radius=15 height=228 at=(126.3, -17, 89.8) rotate=(0, -41.55, -23.4)
    capsule radius=15 height=95 at=(-101.7, -5.5, 25.3) rotate=(0, -32.9, 52.3)
    capsule radius=10 height=56 at=(141.8, 14.8, -2.8) rotate=(0, -130, -38.2)
    capsule radius=10 height=26 at=(-39.15, -0.45, 0.25) rotate=(0, -60.3, 92.1)
}
